{"step_index":185,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-22T10:38:58Z","content":"Priyanshu, you're right — a bare SVG stickman with no images, no voice, no texture is worthless. The test proved exactly what it should have: raw Remotion code alone doesn't produce watchable content. The value of Remotion is as an **assembler and animator of good assets**, not as an asset generator.\n\nLet me save the summary and kill the Remotion studio.","thinking":"Priyanshu is saying:\n\n1. The Remotion test video was bad — no engagement, no voice, just a basic stickman animation. He's right — it was a bare SVG animation with no images, no voice, nothing. Of course it looks terrible.\n\n2. He wants to undo the conversation back to the point where they were choosing the production approach (the screenshot shows the conversation at the \"make test clip\" point).\n\n3. His actual production pipeline decision:\n   - **Remotion** = for fast-paced image-explaining (slideshow-style with transitions, Ken Burns effects on AI-generated images)\n   - **Omni Flash** = for when actual video clips are needed (short AI-generated video segments)\n   - **AI image generation** = the primary visual asset (Nano Banana 2 or similar)\n\nHe also wants a short summary MD file of everything done so far. This hybrid approach makes much more sense: generate high-quality images per scene, animate them in Remotion with pans/zooms/transitions/captions, reserve Omni Flash for moments that truly need motion, and let the voice carry the storytelling. It's essentially my earlier static-images-plus-Ken-Burns recommendation combined with selective video generation.\n\nNow I'll write a concise summary document capturing everything we've accomplished and the decision we landed on.","tool_calls":[{"name":"manage_task","args":{"Action":"list","toolAction":"Listing running tasks","toolSummary":"Task cleanup"}}]}
